# The ledger of `flags`: what Go's `flag` (Go 1.27), Python's `argparse`
# (3.15) and Rust's clap (4.6.7, with clap_complete and clap_mangen) let a
# program do with its arguments, one block per capability, and what Rebel
# answers. Rust's standard library parses no arguments: it hands them over,
# `std::env::args` and `args_os`, which are the language's own `args()` and
# `args_checked()`; the author asked on 2026-11-16 that `flags` be as complete
# as clap, so clap stands on the `rust` lines. The lists are this machine's,
# 2026-11-16: `go doc -all flag`; `dir(argparse)`,
# `dir(argparse.ArgumentParser)` and the signatures `inspect.signature`
# prints; and the names of clap_builder 4.6.7's public functions and enum
# cases in the cargo registry (`Command`, `Arg`, `ArgGroup`, `ArgMatches`,
# `ValueHint`, `ErrorKind`, `ValueSource`), names only. Every expected value of
# the package's tests that Go or Python answers was observed from them the
# same day; clap was not run.

capability flags/command  a declaration of what a program takes, held as a value
  heroes  flags.Command, flags.command, flags.add
  go      NewFlagSet, FlagSet, FlagSet.Init, FlagSet.Name, CommandLine
  rust    clap::Command::new, Command::name, Command::about, Command::author, Command::arg, Command::args, Command::bin_name, Command::display_name, Command::no_binary_name, Arg::new, Arg::id
  python  ArgumentParser, ArgumentParser(prog=, description=)
  note    no declaration is held anywhere but in the program's own value: Go's top-level functions write `CommandLine`, a mutable global the language has none of. The arguments list leaves the program's name out, clap's `no_binary_name`

capability flags/flag  an option of no value, on or off
  heroes  flags.flag, flags.is_on
  go      Bool, BoolVar, FlagSet.Bool, FlagSet.BoolVar
  rust    clap::ArgAction::SetTrue, ArgAction::SetFalse, ArgMatches::get_flag, Arg::short, Arg::long
  python  add_argument(action="store_true"), add_argument(action="store_false"), add_argument(action="store_const", const=)
  note    Go also reads `-v=false`; here a flag takes no value, as argparse and clap refuse one, and turning one off is `with_negation`'s. A flag that stands for a value is `is_on` and the program's own `if`

capability flags/negation  a flag turned off again by `--no-` and its name
  heroes  flags.with_negation
  go      -
  rust    clap::Arg::overrides_with, Arg::overrides_with_all
  python  BooleanOptionalAction
  note    the last of the two given wins, as argparse's does and as clap's two arguments that override each other do

capability flags/count  a flag given several times, counted
  heroes  flags.count_of
  go      BoolFunc, FlagSet.BoolFunc
  rust    clap::ArgAction::Count, ArgMatches::get_count
  python  add_argument(action="count")
  note    `-vvv` counts 3, as argparse counts it; a negation sets the count back to 0

capability flags/option  an option taking a text value, `--name value`, `--name=value`, `-n value`, `-nvalue`, `-n=value`
  heroes  flags.option, flags.value_of
  go      String, StringVar, FlagSet.String, FlagSet.StringVar
  rust    clap::ArgAction::Set, Arg::value_name, Arg::value_names, ArgMatches::get_one, ArgMatches::get_raw, Arg::allow_hyphen_values, Command::allow_hyphen_values
  python  add_argument(action="store"), add_argument(metavar=), add_argument(dest=)
  note    the value is the next argument whatever it is, as Go takes it and clap's `allow_hyphen_values`, so `--offset -5` reads `-5`; argparse refuses a value that opens with `-` and is not a number. `-n=value` reads `value`, as all three read it. An argument is read by its long name, else its short one: no `dest` of another name. Given twice, the last wins

capability flags/repeated  every value of an option given many times, in order
  heroes  flags.values_of
  go      Func, FlagSet.Func
  rust    clap::ArgAction::Append, ArgMatches::get_many, ArgMatches::get_occurrences, ArgMatches::get_raw_occurrences
  python  add_argument(action="append"), add_argument(action="extend"), add_argument(action="append_const")
  note    every option keeps every value given; `value_of` answers the last, so no option is declared repeated or not

capability flags/integer  an option's value read as an integer, refused at the parse where it is not one
  heroes  flags.Kind, flags.with_kind, flags.int_of
  go      Int, IntVar, Int64, Int64Var, Uint, UintVar, Uint64, Uint64Var, FlagSet.Int, FlagSet.IntVar, FlagSet.Int64, FlagSet.Int64Var, FlagSet.Uint, FlagSet.UintVar, FlagSet.Uint64, FlagSet.Uint64Var
  rust    clap::value_parser!(i64), clap::value_parser!(u64)
  python  add_argument(type=int)
  note    read as the language writes an integer (`numbers.parse_int_literal`): Go reads `0x10` and `1_000` as these do, and `007` as octal, which this refuses as the language does; argparse's `int` reads `1_000` and `007` and refuses `0x10`. An unsigned option is `with_range` from 0

capability flags/range  an integer's value held between two bounds
  heroes  flags.with_range
  go      -
  rust    clap::RangedI64ValueParser, clap::RangedU64ValueParser, ValueParser::range
  python  -
  note    both bounds included; a value outside fails `invalid`, naming them

capability flags/float  an option's value read as a decimal number
  heroes  flags.Kind, flags.with_kind, flags.float_of
  go      Float64, Float64Var, FlagSet.Float64, FlagSet.Float64Var
  rust    clap::value_parser!(f64)
  python  add_argument(type=float)
  note    `inf` and `nan` are read, as Go and Python read them

capability flags/duration  an option's value read as a length of time
  heroes  refused: `flags` stands on no `time` (README A1); the value is read as text and given to `time.parse_duration`, whose failure the program reports
  go      Duration, DurationVar, FlagSet.Duration, FlagSet.DurationVar
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    -

