capability flags/converted  an option's value read by a function of the program's own while the arguments are parsed
  heroes  refused: a converter run inside the parse would need a function value per argument kept in the declaration, and a failure of it would need its own code; the program reads `values_of` and converts each in its own code, where its failure is its own
  go      Var, FlagSet.Var, TextVar, FlagSet.TextVar, Value, Getter
  rust    clap::Arg::value_parser, ValueParser, TypedValueParser, ValueParserFactory, value_parser!(PathBuf), value_parser!(OsString)
  python  add_argument(type=), register
  note    -

capability flags/default  the value answered where an argument is not given, and whether it was given
  heroes  flags.with_default, flags.is_given
  go      the `value` argument of every defining function, Flag.DefValue, NFlag, FlagSet.NFlag, Visit, FlagSet.Visit
  rust    clap::Arg::default_value, Arg::default_values, ArgMatches::contains_id
  python  add_argument(default=), set_defaults, get_default, ArgumentParser(argument_default=)
  note    a default is checked against the argument's kind when it is declared, and a wrong one aborts there

capability flags/environment  a value read from an environment variable where the command line leaves it out
  heroes  flags.with_env, flags.parse_with_env, flags.parse_args_with_env
  go      -
  rust    clap::Arg::env, Arg::hide_env, Arg::hide_env_values
  python  -
  note    the environment is handed to the parse, `os.env_all()`'s answer, so that the parse reads no global; a flag's variable is read as a boolean word, `1`, `true`, `yes`, `on`, `y`, `t` or their six opposites and the empty text, in any case; the help always shows the variable's name, never its value

capability flags/source  where an argument's value came from
  heroes  flags.source_of, flags.Source
  go      -
  rust    clap::ArgMatches::value_source, ValueSource::CommandLine, ValueSource::EnvVariable, ValueSource::DefaultValue
  python  -
  note    `absent` is a fourth case where clap answers none

capability flags/required  an option a parse refuses to go without
  heroes  flags.with_required
  go      -
  rust    clap::Arg::required, ErrorKind::MissingRequiredArgument
  python  add_argument(required=)
  note    every missing argument is named in one message, as argparse and clap name them; a value from the environment counts

capability flags/positional  the arguments that are not options, by name and by how many
  heroes  flags.positional, flags.with_arity, flags.Arity
  go      Arg, Args, NArg, FlagSet.Arg, FlagSet.Args, FlagSet.NArg
  rust    clap::Arg::index, Arg::num_args on a positional, Command::allow_missing_positional, Arg::allow_negative_numbers, Command::allow_negative_numbers
  python  add_argument(nargs=), ONE_OR_MORE, OPTIONAL, ZERO_OR_MORE
  note    each positional takes as many as it may while those after it can take their fewest: `A B* C` given 1 2 3 4 is 1, [2 3], 4, and `A? B` given x is none, x, as argparse answers. An argument reading as a negative number is a positional where no short option is a digit, argparse's rule

capability flags/option-of-several  one option taking several values at once, `--point 1 2`
  heroes  refused: an option takes one value each time it is given, so every value stands right after its option and no positional can be taken for one; several values are the option given several times, or one value `with_delimiter` splits
  go      -
  rust    clap::Arg::num_args on an option, Arg::number_of_values, Arg::value_terminator, ErrorKind::TooManyValues, ErrorKind::TooFewValues, ErrorKind::WrongNumberOfValues
  python  add_argument(nargs=) on an option
  note    -

